ChordChart
==========

Detects the chords of a song and prints a bar-aligned chord chart with key, tempo and
time signature. Works on local audio/video files, on video links (YouTube and other
sites supported by yt-dlp) and on Spotify track links (matched to the same recording on
YouTube; see below). Local use only. The chord and key models come from madmom
(model files licensed CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, so non-commercial use only); beats and downbeats
come from Beat This! (CPJKU, MIT) run on onnxruntime, with madmom's DBN.

Status
------
Milestones 1-3 are done (2026-09-26): command line on files and links, download cache,
--start/--end sections, plus the local web page (`chordchart serve`) and the speed-ups
(OpenCV, parallel stages, analysis cache).
Verified by hand (M2 done criterion): the chart for a section of Adele, "Someone Like
You" (YouTube hLQl3WQQoQ0), matched the chords the author knows. Accuracy on a scored
set of songs starts in milestone 4.
Next: M4, the annotation helper and the evaluation harness (see the spec).

Setup (Windows)
---------------
    winget install --id Gyan.FFmpeg -e     # then open a new terminal
    uv sync                                # builds madmom from source (needs MSVC Build Tools)
The lockfile covers Windows and Linux only (see [tool.uv] in pyproject.toml: basic-pitch
and demucs can't be resolved on macOS with Python 3.12 and NumPy 2).

Usage
-----
    uv run chordchart path/to/song.mp3
    uv run chordchart "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=..."
    uv run chordchart "https://open.spotify.com/track/..."
    uv run chordchart song.mp3 --start 1:05 --end 2:10    # just part of the song
    uv run chordchart song.mp3 --format json -o song.json
    uv run chordchart --help

Times in --start/--end are SS, MM:SS or H:MM:SS. Chart and JSON times always refer to
the original song, even when only a section is analysed.

Web page
--------
    uv run chordchart serve                 # opens http://127.0.0.1:8765/ in your browser
    uv run chordchart serve --port 9000 --no-browser
Paste a link (or a file path), optionally a start/end, press Analyze. Progress and the
chart appear on the page. The server only listens on this computer; Ctrl+C stops it.

Notes (main instrument -> piano roll and MIDI)
---------------------------------------------
On the web page, switch to Notes, pick an instrument (Auto = the loudest) and press
Transcribe notes. It uses the link, start and end of the form. The piano roll appears
under the chord sheet, bar lines and chord names lined up; Download MIDI saves the notes.
    uv run chordchart notes "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=..." --start 1:05 --end 1:40
    uv run chordchart notes song.mp3 --instrument bass -o bass.mid   # or .musicxml, .json
Piano roll or Sheet: the Sheet view shows the notes as a piano grand staff (split at
middle C, the chart's key and meter, ties across barlines), drawn in the page by
OpenSheetMusicDisplay, which the app serves itself (chordchart/web/vendor), so it works
offline. Download MusicXML opens in MuseScore, Finale, Sibelius, Dorico and others.
How: Demucs (htdemucs_6s) splits the song into stems; vocals and drums are dropped; the
loudest of bass, guitar, piano and other is transcribed by Spotify's basic-pitch; notes
are snapped to 16ths of the chart's beats, and very short or quiet ones are dropped.
Speed: separating the stems runs on the CPU at roughly the audio's length (a whole
2-minute song ~80 s, a 5-minute one ~4-5 min), so prefer a section. The stems are
cached, so another instrument for the same section takes seconds.
The Demucs model (53 MB, MIT) is downloaded once, on first use, into
%LOCALAPPDATA%\ChordChart\models. Cached stems: %LOCALAPPDATA%\chordchart\cache\stems
(`chordchart cache info|clear` include them).

Pasting YouTube links
---------------------
PowerShell splits an unquoted link at "&". Either quote it, or let chordchart ask:
    uv run chordchart                  # prompts: link, start, end (Enter = whole song)
    uv run chordchart --clipboard      # takes the link you just copied
Full links can be pasted as-is: playlist/radio parameters (&list=, &start_radio=,
&index=, ...) are ignored and only the video is analysed.

Spotify links
-------------
Spotify's audio can't be used: it is DRM-protected, Spotify's Developer Terms forbid
downloading it or feeding it to ML models, and 30 s previews are no longer available to
new apps. So for a track link (open.spotify.com/track/..., spotify:track:...) ChordChart
reads only the track's title, artists and length, searches YouTube for "artist - title",
and analyses the result within 3 s of Spotify's length, preferring the artist's
"<Artist> - Topic" channel (the studio recording). If none is that close it says "no
confident match" instead of guessing. The page shows which video was used, and "Use
this video" takes a YouTube link of your choice instead; ChordChart remembers it.
Albums, playlists, artists, podcasts and spotify.link short links are refused with a
message saying so.
Track details come from the Spotify Web API when a key is set up, else from the public
track page. A key is optional (free, https://developer.spotify.com/dashboard, "Web API"):
    set SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID and SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET, or put them in
    %LOCALAPPDATA%\ChordChart\settings.json as
    {"spotify_client_id": "...", "spotify_client_secret": "..."}
Matches are cached in cache\downloads\spotify.json; Spotify's details in it are
refreshed after 30 days (their terms allow temporary caching only).

Caches
------
Links are downloaded once and reused; running the same link again needs no network.
The models' results are cached too, so re-analysing the same song (and section) takes
a couple of seconds instead of the full analysis.
    Location:  %LOCALAPPDATA%\chordchart\cache\downloads   (audio)
               %LOCALAPPDATA%\chordchart\cache\analysis    (model results)
               (set CHORDCHART_CACHE_DIR to use another folder; --help shows the path)
    uv run chordchart cache info       # locations, counts, sizes
    uv run chordchart cache clear      # delete downloads and cached analyses
    uv run chordchart <link> --refresh # download again and re-run the models

Speed
-----
Beat tracking runs at the same time as chords and key (4 threads). On a 4-core laptop a
song takes about 10 s (2 min) to 16 s (6 min) the first time, and about 1 s again
(cached). Beat This!'s network is most of that; experiments/profile/ has the profilers.
`chordchart serve` keeps the models loaded between analyses.

If YouTube downloads start failing, upgrade yt-dlp:
    uv lock --upgrade-package yt-dlp && uv sync

Windows desktop app
-------------------
A self-contained app for people without Python: double-click the icon, the page opens
in the default browser; "Quit ChordChart" (or ~5 minutes with no page open) exits.
Everything is bundled: Python, madmom and its models, the Beat This! model and
onnxruntime, OpenCV, an LGPL ffmpeg, yt-dlp
and its JavaScript runtime (deno). Licenses: packaging/build/<variant>/licenses after a
build, and the "Licenses" link in the app's footer.
Two variants (packaging/variants.py); both can be installed side by side:
    lite  "ChordChart"        chords only                505 MB, zip 221 MB
    full  "ChordChart Notes"  chords + notes (torch...)  1.1 GB, zip 430 MB
The app notices which one it is at startup and shows the Chords/Notes toggle only in
the full one.
    uv sync --group desktop                             # PyInstaller (+ pywebview, later)
    winget install JRSoftware.InnoSetup                 # for the installer
    uv run python packaging/build.py                    # lite -> packaging/out/ChordChart-*.zip
    uv run python packaging/build.py --variant full     # -> ChordChartNotes-*.zip
    uv run python packaging/build.py --installer        # also the *-Setup-*.exe (either variant)
    uv run python packaging/sandbox/run_sandbox_test.py zip        # test lite on a clean
    uv run python packaging/sandbox/run_sandbox_test.py installer  # Windows (Sandbox)
The full build needs several GB of free memory (PyInstaller analyses torch).
The packaged app has a headless check: ChordChart.exe --self-test report.json
(in lite, the notes checks are reported as skipped).

Tests
-----
    uv run pytest                          # fast tests (no models, no network)
    uv run pytest -m "slow or not slow"    # plus the model tests
    uv run pytest -m network               # real YouTube downloads

